  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Scattered thunderstorms, some of which could be \nsevere late Thursday, with a few hit and miss thunderstorms possible \nagain Friday afternoon. \n\nA decent set-up for some strong to severe storms on Thursday, albeit \nbetter to our west where the better forcing is more likely to \noverlap the favorable dynamics. Even so, especially for western \nMA/CT, particularly along and west of the Connecticut river valley, \nsome severe storms are certainly possible in the Thursday afternoon \nto midnight time frame. This comes courtesy of a marginally deep \nupper trough that becomes slightly negatively tilted as it digs into \nthe northeast. The trough axis remains west of SNE on Thursday into \nFriday placing us beneath moist SW flow advecting in PWATS of 1.5-2\" \nand dewpoints in the upper 60s. This moist flow in the warm sector \nwill lead to potential for localized downpours some of which, could \ncause localized flooding. There is a marginal risk (level 1 of 4) \nfor flash flooding in western/central MA and CT. This is in addition \nto the marginal severe risk (level 1 of 5) thanks to the 1000-2000 \nJ/kg CAPE and 0-6km bulk shear. As previously mentioned, the slower \nfrontal passage means the better forcing will be to our west, so the \nbest chance of damaging storms is west of the CT river valley. The \ngreatest threat is from straight line winds and hail, but 0-1km SRH \nvalues 150-200m2s2 mean a tornado can't be ruled out. By Friday the \nfront moves through and some scattered afternoon thunderstorms are \npossible before a much drier airmass drops in. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Mainly dry and seasonable weather follows for this \nweekend with comfortable humidity. \n\nHigh pressure builds in behind the departing shortwave/cold\nfront for this weekend. The result will be mainly dry and seasonable \nweather Sat into Sun with comfortable humidity. High temps will \nmainly be in the upper 70s and 80s with lows in the 50s to the lower \n60s. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...A period or two of unsettled weather may return \nearly next week with a backdoor cold front in the vicinity of \nsouthern New England. Large spread/high uncertainty on temps \ndependent on backdoor cold front/s location.\n\nQuite a tricky forecast for the first half of next week with a \nbackdoor cold front in the vicinity. If we end up on the northern \nside of the front...it will be cool with highs in the upper 60s and \n70s. If we end up south of the backdoor cold front...highs may reach \n85-90+. Given the uncertainty we blended the guidance. Current \nthinking is we may be on the warmer side Mon and then cooler Tue. It \ndoes seem a decent shot the backdoor cold front temporarily drops \nsouth into at least part of our region on Tue. But again...high \nuncertainty on temperatures in the Mon through Wed time frame. \n\nThere also will be the risk for some showers at times in the Mon \nthrough Wed time frame. While we are not expecting a complete \nwashout...thinking a round or two scattered showers and perhaps a \nfew thunderstorms is reasonable. Timing though remains quite \nuncertain at this point.",
